course: dictionary
lesson: 14958
title: Can you trust it?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Reliability is a noun. It tells you how much you can trust someone or something to do what it should, every time. It comes from the word reliable: a reliable friend is one who turns up when they promise.
explain: «My bike is reliable: it starts every single morning.» The sentence does not use the word reliability, but it shows it. The bike works every time, so you can trust it. Reliability is the amount of that trust.
explain: In science, reliability has a close meaning. «Ruby repeated her experiment three times and got almost the same results each time.» Her results showed good reliability, because they stayed the same when she repeated the test.
fact: Reliability is a noun, so you can say «the reliability of the bus service».
fact: Reliability means how much you can trust someone or something to do what it should, every time.
fact: In science, the reliability of a test is how much the results stay the same when you repeat it.
fact: Scientists repeat measurements and take the average, because results that stay similar show good reliability.
fact: Useful phrases: «reliability of results», «improve reliability», «known for its reliability».
exercise: «The bus is known for its reliability, and it almost always arrives on time.» What does reliability mean here? | *How much you can trust the bus to come when it should | How much the bus costs | How many seats the bus has | How fast the bus drives
exercise: «Ruby repeated her experiment and got similar results each time.» What does this show about her test? | *Good reliability, because the results stay the same | The results changed each time | It is a very loud test | Nothing at all, because she only did it once
exercise: «Our new kettle is known for its reliability.» Which word in this sentence is the noun? | known | *reliability | kettle | its
test: «Dad says the car has great reliability, because it has never broken down.» What does reliability mean? | Size | *Trust that the car will work when it is needed | Speed | Colour
test: Which step would improve the reliability of an experiment? | Using a shorter ruler | Writing the results in pencil | Changing the test each time | *Repeating the test several times and taking the average
exam: Mia says, «I trust my friend Sam because he is always on time, so he has real reliability.» What is reliability? | How quickly someone runs | How many friends someone has | *How much you can trust someone to do what they should, every time | How tall someone is
